# Intro
A stabilized shear layer (two counter-streaming gas streams, no seed
perturbation) carrying two seeded Gaussian FUV/LW blobs, one per stream.
Measures whether the LW/FUV hyperbolic propagation and dissipation scheme
introduces a directional artifact under a persistent relative velocity
across a contact discontinuity, using an exact reflection symmetry the
continuum initial condition possesses but the discrete scheme need not
preserve.

## The shear layer
The velocity profile is the `KelvinHelmholtzGrowthRate_3D` two-interface
profile with the seed perturbation removed and a `tanh` transition in place
of the usual piecewise ramp; the layer is wide enough that the box-scale
Kelvin-Helmholtz mode sits at the layer's own stability cutoff, so the flow
stays laminar over the run instead of rolling up. `isrf_shear_asymmetry_check.py`
reports a KH-contamination control (`kh`, `drho`) every run; a run whose
layer destabilizes has its asymmetry metrics declared void rather than
silently trusted.

## The seeded field, and why there is no star
Two Gaussian `FUVSpecificEnergy`/`LWSpecificEnergy` blobs are written
directly into the IC (`--pulse-amplitude`/`--pulse-sigma-h`), one centred in
each stream. This isolates the propagation and dissipation operators from
injection, the star's own timestep, and any star-gas relative velocity
entirely -- there is no star particle in this example at all. The specific
flux field is not an IC-readable quantity, so every run starts from `F = 0`;
this is a well-posed initial condition, not a limitation. The two blob
positions are chosen so the `variant=shear` initial condition has an exact
discrete symmetry (a reflection plus a half-box shift) that the true
solution must respect for all time, since the radiation subsystem itself
contains no gas velocity; every gated metric measures how much the discrete
scheme violates that symmetry.

## Metallicity
As in `ISRFPropagationSpeedSweep`: `initial_metallicity` defaults to 0.05 so
the seeded blobs are not absorbed away before there is anything left to
measure.

## Cooling
`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`, as in the sibling
examples: gas thermodynamics play no role in what this example measures.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
Override any parameter via the matching env var: `variant` (`shear` or
`contrast`), `source_geometry` (`blobs` or `slab`), `v_shear` (km/s, full
relative velocity between the two streams), `layer_width_h`,
`density_ratio` (contrast variant only), `bulk_temperature_K`,
`pulse_amplitude`/`pulse_sigma_h`, `c_hyp_margin`, `c_hyp_pin`
(`LW_FUV_c_hyp_pin_for_debugging`), `alpha_max`/`alpha_pin`
(`LW_FUV_dissipation_alpha_max`/`..._pin_for_debugging`, debug/test only,
never use in a production run), `initial_metallicity`, `time_end`,
`dt_max`, `dt_min`, `delta_time`, and thread count (`n_threads`); see
`run.sh` for the full list and defaults. `run_name=<dir> ./run.sh` moves a
run's outputs into `<dir>`.

Before running the full sweep, generate the IC alone and check the
population imbalance between the two blobs
(`python3 makeIC.py --variant shear --source-geometry blobs -o ic.hdf5`,
see its own printed `Population imbalance` line): if the `(m*u)`-weighted
imbalance exceeds 1%, the reflection-symmetry metrics below have
insufficient resolving power at that resolution and `--pulse-sigma-h` or
`--level` must be raised before trusting any run.

# Checking
```
python3 isrf_shear_asymmetry_check.py --variant $variant --source-geometry $source_geometry --v-shear $v_shear --c-hyp-pin $c_hyp_pin --c-hyp-margin $c_hyp_margin
python3 shear_compare.py --mode gate --runs dir1 dir2 ... --zero zero_shear_dir
python3 shear_compare.py --mode pair --plus plus_dir --minus minus_dir
```
`run.sh` runs the per-run check automatically, computing `A_centroid`,
`A_spread`, and `A_energy` (the reflection-symmetry violation, `shear`
variant only) plus the KH-contamination and configuration-echo metrics.

The gate is self-calibrating, not an absolute threshold: a sheared run
passes a given metric if `|A_k| <= max(3*|A_k(0)|, floor)`, where `A_k(0)`
is the same metric measured in the matching zero-shear control run in the
same sweep. The factor of 3 and the floors (0.01 for all three metrics) are
declared conventions, not derived bounds -- wide enough that ordinary
glass-noise scatter between two runs does not trip the gate, narrow enough
to catch an effect at the percent level. `shear_compare.py --mode gate`
performs this comparison; a run flagged void by the KH-contamination control
is excluded from the gate rather than trusted.

The `contrast` variant (no exact symmetry) and the `slab` source geometry
are report-only: `--mode pair` compares a `+v_shear`/`-v_shear` pair via a
grid-interpolated field, with no threshold; the slab geometry's
`A_xstructure` (x-direction structure in a field that should stay
x-independent) is likewise reported without a gate.
